WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.528
Picture an office with three clerks, each with a full copy of the account files.

2
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.441
For the busy season, a fourth clerk is hired.

3
00:00:10.441 --> 00:00:16.368
She takes over a share of the alphabet, and copies those files from the colleagues handing them over.

4
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:19.224
Soon the office runs out of cabinet space.

5
00:00:19.224 --> 00:00:23.035
Nobody told the old clerks to shred the files they gave away.

6
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:27.637
Cassandra, the database where no machine is in charge, does the same.

7
00:00:27.637 --> 00:00:31.911
When a new node joins, the old ones keep every row they handed over.

8
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:37.683
And the join ends with a log line saying the new node is NORMAL, which sounds like the end.

9
00:00:37.683 --> 00:00:41.237
So what actually moves, from where, and what's left behind?

10
00:00:41.667 --> 00:00:43.442
Here's the lab cluster.

11
00:00:43.442 --> 00:00:53.093
Three nodes, and three copies of every row, so each node holds all of it: 50,000 ledger entries, 1.16 megabytes on each disk.

12
00:00:53.367 --> 00:01:00.563
Each node has 16 tokens, its places on the ring, and a range is the stretch of ring between two tokens.

13
00:01:00.833 --> 00:01:03.805
Every node owns all of it: a hundred percent.

14
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:12.512
cass4 starts with the same cluster name, cass1 as its seed, empty data folders, and its own address not in the seed list.

15
00:01:12.767 --> 00:01:17.893
That last part means it will bootstrap: copy its share of the data on its first start.

16
00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:22.023
It asks the seed for the map, and learns all 48 tokens.

17
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:26.640
It picks 16 tokens of its own and announces them by gossip.

18
00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.951
Everyone now shows it as UJ: up, and joining.

19
00:01:30.233 --> 00:01:39.285
From this moment, every new write for the ranges it's about to own gets a copy sent to cass4 too, so nothing written during the copy is missed.

20
00:01:39.533 --> 00:01:42.911
Then it waits 30 seconds, so the whole cluster has heard.

21
00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:44.321
Now the copy.

22
00:01:44.321 --> 00:01:46.440
cass4 takes over 47 ranges.

23
00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:49.423
For each one, three nodes have a copy.

24
00:01:49.423 --> 00:01:51.779
Which one should it copy from?

25
00:01:51.779 --> 00:01:53.113
Does any copy do?

26
00:01:53.113 --> 00:01:54.369
Pause and guess.

27
00:01:57.567 --> 00:02:04.699
Cassandra insists on one: the replica it replaces, the node that held that range before and won't afterwards.

28
00:02:04.967 --> 00:02:05.773
Here's why.

29
00:02:05.773 --> 00:02:13.620
Say a write was confirmed by cass1 and cass2 only: two of three, a majority, so the app was told it's safe.

30
00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.862
cass4 is taking cass1's place for that range.

31
00:02:16.862 --> 00:02:22.127
If it copied from cass3, which missed the write, only cass2 would still have it.

32
00:02:22.333 --> 00:02:27.481
A later read that asks cass3 and cass4 would miss a write the app was promised.

33
00:02:27.867 --> 00:02:38.455
So it copies 16 ranges from cass1, 16 from cass2 and 15 from cass3, about 280 kilobytes from each, all at the same time.

34
00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:40.264
This is called streaming.

35
00:02:40.633 --> 00:02:43.767
cass4 switches to NORMAL, and shows UN.

36
00:02:43.767 --> 00:02:49.713
It owns 73.2 percent of the data, and the old nodes about 75 percent each.

37
00:02:49.967 --> 00:02:51.708
But look at their disks.

38
00:02:51.708 --> 00:02:53.232
Still 1.16 megabytes.

39
00:02:53.232 --> 00:02:58.093
Cassandra never deletes data just because a node stopped owning it.

40
00:02:58.367 --> 00:03:04.057
So three quarters of the rows now sit on four disks, and only three of those copies count.

41
00:03:04.057 --> 00:03:06.017
The office's cabinets, exactly.

42
00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:09.631
The shredding is one command, nodetool cleanup.

43
00:03:09.631 --> 00:03:14.428
On each old node, it rewrites its files without the ranges it gave away.

44
00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:18.523
cass1 went from 5,000 partitions to 3,729.

45
00:03:18.523 --> 00:03:24.804
What each node streamed to cass4 is exactly what its cleanup deleted.

46
00:03:25.067 --> 00:03:29.405
Here the office picture breaks: a clerk would notice the full cabinet.

47
00:03:29.405 --> 00:03:30.520
A node never does.

48
00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:31.746
A few rules.

49
00:03:31.746 --> 00:03:39.852
Run cleanup after the last new node has joined, on one old node at a time, because rewriting files costs disk work.

50
00:03:40.133 --> 00:03:48.033
Join one node at a time, and wait for UN, because two joins at once would each plan against a ring that's about to change.

51
00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:54.396
And expect hours, not seconds: the lab join took 90 seconds, a real node takes hours.

52
00:03:54.396 --> 00:04:00.921
Removing a node is a join in reverse: it streams its ranges to the new owners, then leaves.

53
00:04:01.367 --> 00:04:03.079
So a join is not one step.

54
00:04:03.079 --> 00:04:10.916
Learn the map, pick tokens, take new writes, copy each range from the node it replaces, and only then switch to NORMAL.

55
00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:14.552
And the old copies stay until someone runs cleanup.

56
00:04:14.552 --> 00:04:16.041
Nothing tidies itself.

57
00:04:16.633 --> 00:04:17.784
One quick check.

58
00:04:17.784 --> 00:04:19.511
Next week you add cass5.

59
00:04:19.511 --> 00:04:22.677
When do you run cleanup, and on which nodes?

60
00:04:22.677 --> 00:04:23.828
Pause and think.

61
00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:31.383
After cass5 shows UN, on every old node, one at a time.
